mmittee":"Full Council","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://tunbridgewells.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-22","committee":"Cabinet","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://tunbridgewells.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-09","committee":"Finance, Innovation and Transformation Cabinet Advisory Board","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://tunbridgewells.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-10","committee":"Communities Housing and Environment Cabinet Advisory Board","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://tunbridgewells.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-11","committee":"Full Council","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://tunbridgewells.moderngov.co.uk"}],"recent":[{"date":"2026-09-07","committee":"Finance, Innovation and Transformation Cabinet Advisory Board","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://tunbridgewells.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-08","committee":"Communities Housing and Environment Cabinet Advisory Board","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://tunbridgewells.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-15","committee":"Audit and Governance Committee","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://tunbridgewells.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-21","committee":"Overview and Scrutiny Committee","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://tunbridgewells.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-24","committee":"Cabinet","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://tunbridgewells.moderngov.co.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Tunbridge Wells council starts year with stronger cash position as investment income outperforms","lead":"Tunbridge Wells Cabinet heard on 24 September that the council opened the financial year with \u00a341.9 million in cash, \u00a31.9 million more than budgeted, after capital projects totalling \u00a314.074 million were rescheduled into 2026/27. The stronger liquidity position has boosted investment returns. The council forecasts investment income of \u00a32.2 million for the year against a \u00a32 million budget, a surplus of \u00a3200,000 that will be transferred to reserves, after achieving an average interest rate of 4.13 per cent rather than the budgeted 3.94 per cent. Q1 investment balances reached \u00a348.3 million, well above the year-end budget assumption of \u00a328.9 million. The council holds no external debt. However, the capital programme is forecast to spend \u00a319.9 million in 2026/27 against a budget of \u00a38.9 million\u2014more than double\u2014and the Grant Volatility Reserve, which buffers against swings in government funding, is forecast to be completely depleted by March 2027.","iso":"2026-09-24","paper_title":"Quarter 1 Treasury Management 202627","paper_url":"https://tunbridgewells.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s84985/84985.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Appendix D Usable Reserves Q1 202627","paper_url":"https://tunbridgewells.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s85000/85000.pdf","headline":"Successor unitary authority will open on 1 April 2028 with net usable reserves of \u00a31,150k.","detail":"Total usable reserves forecast for 2027/28 are \u00a37,276k. After deducting commitments for 2027/28 (\u00a34,377k), restricted grants not available for Tunbridge Wells' own spending (\u00a31,984k), internal borrowing requiring repayment (\u00a33,687k), and commitments beyond 1 April 2028 (\u00a32,439k), the net position handed to the successor unitary is \u00a31,150k.","category":"governance","matters":"The opening financial position of a new unitary authority directly determines its financial resilience and capacity to absorb unexpected costs or service pressures during establishment.","figures":"Net position handed to unitary: \u00a31,150k (from total reserves \u00a37,276k)","quote":"NET POSITION HANDED TO UNITARY 1 APRIL 2028 1,150","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Appendix D Usable Reserves Q1 202627","paper_url":"https://tunbridgewells.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s85000/85000.pdf","headline":"Internal borrowing of \u00a33,687k remains unfunded, with no identified financing mechanism in budget allocations.","detail":"The council has \u00a33,687k of internal borrowing (reserves lent to capital and revenue projects) for which projects have not yet been financed through the budget or mainstream funding mechanisms. The paper labels this as 'projects not yet financed/MRP', indicating the capital/revenue work is done but repayment mechanisms are undefined.","category":"borrowing","matters":"Unfinanced internal borrowing represents a deferred claim on future budgets or the unitary authority's finances, creating refinancing risk and potential constraint on future service spending.","figures":"Internal Borrowing: \u00a33,687k","quote":"Internal Borrowing still to be repaid (projects not yet financed/MRP) (3,687)","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Appendix D Usable Reserves Q1 202627","paper_url":"https://tunbridgewells.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s85000/85000.pdf","headline":"Grant Volatility Reserve of \u00a32,001k planned for complete depletion to zero by 31 March 2027.","detail":"The reserve buffer against government grant income variation is forecasted to be drawn completely from opening balance of \u00a32,001k, with no receipts planned and payments of \u00a32,001k, leaving a nil closing balance by end of financial year.","category":"reserves","matters":"A council without a grant volatility buffer faces increased exposure to unexpected variations in government funding, particularly during structural transition to unitary status.","figures":"Grant Volatility Reserve: opening \u00a32,001k, receipts \u00a30, payments \u00a32,001k, closing \u00a30","quote":"Grant Volatility 2,001 0 (2,001) 0","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Q1 202627 Appendix C","paper_url":"https://tunbridgewells.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s84988/84988.pdf","headline":"Council holds no external debt, with all long-term liabilities limited to S106 repayment obligations of \u00a38.6m.","detail":"The Council's operational and authorised boundaries for external debt are both set at zero. The Council's only recorded long-term liabilities (\u00a38.626m) relate to Section 106 contributions with repayment conditions, not external borrowing. This zero-debt position indicates strong financial independence.","category":"borrowing","matters":"Absence of external debt is a significant strength for a lending decision. It signals the Council has financed its capital programme through reserves rather than borrowing, indicating financial self-sufficiency and reducing refinancing risk.","figures":"External debt: \u00a30; S106 liabilities: \u00a38,626,000","quote":"Operational boundary for external debt \u00a30... Authorised limit for external debt \u00a30","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Q1 202627 Appendix C","paper_url":"https://tunbridgewells.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s84988/84988.pdf","headline":"Investment income is tracking ahead of budget, with \u00a32.2m projected against \u00a32m budgeted.","detail":"The Council is generating investment returns better than anticipated. Year-to-date performance of \u00a3554k in Q1 annualizes to approximately \u00a32.2m, in line with or exceeding the full-year forecast of \u00a32.2m against the \u00a32m budget. This outperformance improves available resources for capital or revenue support.","category":"income","matters":"Investment income performance better than budget improves the Council's funding flexibility and reduces pressure on reserves or borrowing. Lenders benefit from evidence that interest assumptions are conservative.","figures":"\u00a32,200,000 (projected) vs \u00a32,000,000 (budgeted); \u00a3554,000 (year-to-date)","quote":"Investment Income including investments and bank interest \u00a32,000,000 \u00a32,200,000 projected... \u00a3554,000","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-24"},"name":"Tunbridge Wells","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-25","since_iso":"2026-08-26","distress":false,"headline":"Successor unitary authority will open on 1 April 2028 with net usable reserves of \u00a31,150k.","lead":"The opening financial position of a new unitary authority directly determines its financial resilience and capacity to absorb unexpected costs or service pressures during establishment.","also":["38 other findings in this window"],"watch":"Finance, Innovation and Transformation Cabinet Advisory Board on 2026-10-05","n_findings":39,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"Appendix D Usable Reserves Q1 202627","top_paper_url":"https://tunbridgewells.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s85000/85000.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":153,"news_drafts_total":1}}